953125, 0.0168914794921875, -0.010589599609375, -0.0179901123046875, -0.00893402099609375, 0.0208892822265625, 0.0027523040771484375, -0.00849151611328125, 0.048004150390625, -0.0238494873046875, 0.0190887451171875, 0.0194244384765625, 0.0086822509765625, 0.03314208984375, 0.0219573974609375, 0.017913818359375, -0.0181732177734375, 0.043487548828125, -0.0016021728515625, 0.01163482666015625, -0.0092926025390625, 0.0204620361328125, -0.005153656005859375, -0.0140228271484375, 0.0019445419311523438, 0.00016415119171142578, -0.01654052734375, 0.00565338134765625, 0.049346923828125, -0.00511932373046875, -0.01715087890625, -0.04132080078125, 0.019561767578125, -0.01947021484375, -0.0005764961242675781, 0.03692626953125, 0.0193939208984375, 0.006008148193359375, -0.015289306640625, -0.00571441650390625, -0.00266265869140625, -0.0037555694580078125, -0.03057861328125, 0.0267333984375, -0.0236968994140625, 0.0014886856079101562, -0.0181732177734375, -0.0156707763671875, 0.020172119140625, 0.004329681396484375, 0.004878997802734375, -0.0184478759765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines\u2014such as those underlying LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022) and similar large benchmarks\u2014optimize individual, task-specific datasets in isolation. This approach restricts systematic transfer of supervision between related generative capabilities, limits efficient organization of multimodal data, and makes unified progress across tasks difficult. A key unresolved challenge is how to curate and organize datasets to enable cross-capability transfer while faithfully capturing dependencies among generative skills.\n\n**Our contributions are:**\n1. A unified, capability-driven data infrastructure for large-scale multimodal model training, introducing precise definitions and alignment of three foundational generative capability types: text-image grounding, inter-image transformation (editing), and image-knowledge association.  \n2. Concrete curriculum scheduling methods leveraging empirically-observed task dependencies, including explicit construction and validation of a capability dependency graph for optimal training sequence.  \n3. Technical advances in large-scale data filtering, annotation, and bias mitigation, incorporating granular QA protocols and measurable demographic balancing.  \n4. Comprehensive empirical evaluation with ablations and error analysis, demonstrating the measurable impact of each data engine, curriculum ordering, and bias mitigation protocol on multimodal generation benchmarks.\n\nFigure and table references correspond to respective sections in the appendix or supplementary materials.\n\nWe address the challenge of dataset organization for cross-capability transfer with a **capability-driven data infrastructure**, a unified framework for large-scale multimodal model training. **Generative capabilities** in our context refer to distinct functional skills a model acquires: (1) \"Text-image grounding\" (ability to associate texts with semantically matched images), (2) \"Inter-image transformation\" (modifying images in specified, trackable ways, i.e., editing), and (3) \"Image-knowledge association\" (linking images with factual or ontological entities). Figure 1 (see appendix) summarizes our system of three core data engines\u2014**Text-Image Grounding**, **Inter-Image Transformation**, and **Image-Knowledge Association**\u2014which feed into a curriculum scheduler supervising model training. The main components, data flows, and roles are detailed in Table 1 (see supplementary materials).\n\n**Capability-specific supervision** entails tailoring supervision formats, modalities, and annotation schemas to match each capability type. For example, editing tasks\u2014such as turning a \"daytime cityscape\" into \"nighttime\"\u2014demand before/after image pairs with verified transformation labels, while knowledge association labels images by unambiguous Wikidata or ImageNet entity IDs, e.g. \"Bruges Belfry (Q2364).\" Capability boundaries are mapped to dataset and annotation structures to prevent cross-contamination and enable controlled transfer learning.\n\n**Capability-aligned curriculum scheduling** sequences training tasks based on empirically derived dependency graphs. To determine training order, we hold out downstream tasks (e.g., factual grounding, complex editing) and train model variants on subsets limited to foundational capabilities (e.g., text-image grounding only). Tasks included as downstream in our analysis are: compositional image synthesis, multi-step image editing, and fine-grained entity retrieval. For each, zero-shot transferability is assessed using retrieval accuracy, Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID), targeted edit classification accuracy, and entity-level micro-precision, averaged over three stratified validation splits (val: 10M text-image pairs, 10M edit pairs, 2M image-entity pairs). The dependency graph is constructed by observing which upstream capabilities yield the largest improvements for each held-out downstream task. Greedy curriculum search and ablation tests on task inclusion are used to identify optimal sequencing. Data frequencies are dynamically increased in response to observed transfer plateaus or unexplained validation error spikes.\n\nOur three interoperable data engines operate as follows:\n- **Text-Image Grounding:** Sources large web crawls (filtered for explicit content and near-duplicates), internal domain-specific image repositories, and crowdsourced in-domain sets. Deduplication uses CLIP similarity (>0.95 threshold) and presence of overlapping descriptors. Recaptioning employs a mixture of state-of-the-art ensemble captioners and crowd annotators, filtered by language fluency and domain expertise. Human annotators are recruited via specialty crowdsourcing platforms, pass English comprehension (>98%, 30-item test) and image relevance exams, then receive calibration on 50-item expert-verified gold sets (95%+ to proceed). Monthly, >5% of annotation batches are double-blindly reviewed; agreement above Cohen's \u03ba=0.89 is continuously tracked. Each caption is labeled for scene, object, and attribute granularity to support downstream compositional evaluation.\n- **Inter-Image Transformation:** Aggregates from public editing benchmarks (e.g., CelebA-HQ Edits, ImageNet-AE), algorithmic augmentations (neural style transfer, geometric edits, color transfer), and non-commercial user-generated datasets. Edit saliency classifiers (ResNet-50 trained on edit-detection, 94% ROC-AUC) flag trivial or ambiguous edits for removal. Random manual audits (5k pairs/sample) clear only those with >90% edit recognizability agreement. For synthetic generation, Gaussian noise, color jitter, and morph-based transformations are tuned to retain recognizability as judged by edit-classifier score thresholds.\n- **Image-Knowledge Association:** Uses entity linking tools (e.g., BLINK, TagMe) and linked structured corpora (Wikidata, ImageNet) to assign entity labels. Disambiguation arises in multi-label or referential ambiguity cases, for which annotators (n=5 per ambiguous instance) select the majority-voted label. Entities per batch must meet a minimum unique count (e.g., \u22651000 per 10k batch). Cross-entity correlation matrices are monitored to avoid overfitting to common classes. Monthly, failure analysis examines random 2k ambiguous labelings.\n\n**Bias mitigation** applies three main protocols:  \n1. **Subgroup Definition and Coverage**: We use demographic (e.g., gender, race per FairFace classes) and object attribute subgroups (Balanced ImageNet categories). Annotated subgroup definitions follow standardized taxonomies; e.g., FairFace's seven race/ethnicity categories and gender split.  \n2. **Thresholds and Rebalancing Criteria**: Subgroups with below-median (<5%) representation, or deviations >2 SD from balanced ratio are upsampled. Bias is quantitatively evaluated by tracking unique entity ratio, gender/race class entropy, and edit diversity in validation batches.  \n3. **Synthetic Augmentation**: For rare subgroups, new images are generated via GAN-based synthesis (StyleGAN2), attribute swapping, and controlled scene composition (image blending with verified object insertion). Bias audits (random 10k/image class sample) measure group-wise F1 scores before and after intervention. Evaluation shows increases in underrepresented group accuracy from 79.2%\u219287.1% post-rebalancing.\n\nTo ensure annotation consistency and high quality, **caption expert** selection pools candidates via verified crowdsourcing platforms. Annotators are required to pass a two-stage screening: Stage 1 is a standardized language reasoning exam (30 Q, 98%+ required); Stage 2 is task-specific, using 50 gold-standard caption/item pairs with threshold 95%+ accuracy. Regularized calibration on confusion items and drift checks (>5% monthly spot-checks, double-blind evaluation) maintain quality. Error profiles are logged (e.g., lexical ambiguity, missing attributes) for retraining or protocol updates.\n\nOur infrastructure compiles: 440M captioned T2I pairs (train: 420M, val: 10M, test: 10M), 120M editing pairs (105M/10M/5M), and 27M image-entity pairs (24M/2M/1M). After filtering, ~13% of content is removed (e.g., for inappropriate/low-confidence or duplicate entries). Aggregate annotation agreement rates exceed 90% across all sets (\u03ba=0.85\u20130.91); Table 2 (see appendix) presents per-subset statistics.\n\nWe perform extensive ablations to assess the impact of each core method (see supplementary). Disabling curriculum scheduling yields a mean 3.5% drop in CLIP accuracy; ablation of Inter-Image Transformation reduces edit accuracy by 7.3%; omitting bias rebalancing lowers subgroup F1 by up to 8% on underrepresented groups. Table 3 (see appendix) presents primary results for our 3B and 6B parameter diffusion models, compared to LAION-5B-trained baselines. Breakdown:  \n- **CLIP-based retrieval/accuracy** computed using test splits (10M) over composite prompts;  \n- **FID** calculated with official 50k sample protocol, applied to val/test splits;  \n- **Editing accuracy** defined as correct edit retrieval on targeted edit descriptions (edit match attested by expert-labeled pairs, n=10k);  \n- **Entity micro-precision** computed on per-image entity retrieval with 1:1 correspondence to gold labels in the test set.\n\nQualitative results (Figure 2, appendix) demonstrate generalization from prompts (\"a red truck in heavy snow\") to context-consistent edits (\"remove snow\"), but persistent errors remain in: (a) rare composite entity rendering, e.g., \"two persons in traditional Sami dress,\" where generated attire is often stereotyped; (b) fine attribute swaps, especially for ambiguous geometric edits; (c) image-knowledge association for rarely-labeled entities (<0.1% in corpus), which show up to 15% micro-precision gap versus common entities.\n\n**Limitations:** (1) Long-tail entity coverage remains insufficient for precise knowledge association; our largest models underperform by up to 12% for the rarest 5% of entities. (2) Despite bias reduction"}}